In France, elections have traditionally been a not very technological experience: voters isolate themselves in a booth and put inside an envelope a pre-printed sheet of paper with the candidate they have chosen.
After those responsible verify the identity of the voter, the voter drops the envelope inside the ballot box and signs the ballot sheet.
French electoral law strictly codifies procedures.
Since 1988, ballot boxes must be transparent so that voters and observers can witness that there are no envelopes at the beginning of the vote and that there are no other envelopes that are not those of the electors duly numbered and authorized.
Candidates can send representatives to testify to each part of the procedure. In the evening, volunteers count the votes under strong oversight, following specific procedures.
