When a small group of living beings (a small population) is separated from the main and original group (e.g., crossing a mountain range or a river, or emigrating to a new island and cannot go back) they will often find themselves in an environment other than they had before.
This new environment has different resources and competitors, so that the new population will need characteristics or adaptations different from those I needed before to be a strong competitor.
The original population has not changed at all, they still need the same adaptations as before.
Over time, as the new population begins to adapt to its new environment, it begins to look less and less like the other population.
Finally, after thousands or even millions of years, the two populations were so different that they could no longer be considered the same species.
To this process we call speciation, which means the formation of new species. Speciation is a consequence that cannot be avoided and a very important part of evolution.
