In 1624, the Dutch East India Company established a base in south-eastern Taiwan, where it began the transformation of indigenous grain production practices and resorted to Chinese workers for rice and sugar plantations.
In 1683, Qing Dynasty forces (1644–1912) took control of the coastal areas of western and northern Taiwan, declaring Taiwan a province of the Qing empire in 1885.
In 1895, after the defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895), the Qing government signed the Shimonoseki treaty, by which it ceded Taiwan's sovereignty to Japan, which ruled the island until 1945.
