Technological determinism is a term that in practice encompasses a wide range of ideas, from the technological thrust or the technological imperative to a strict sense that human destiny is guided by a hidden logic that is associated with scientific laws and their manifestation in technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share two general ideas: that the development of technology itself follows a path largely beyond cultural or political influence, and that technology, at the same time, has "effects" on societies that are inherent, rather than socially conditioned.
For example, it could be said that motor cars necessarily lead to the development of roads.
However, an all-country road network for a handful of cars is not economically viable, so new production methods are being developed to reduce the cost of having a car.
The possession of mass cars also leads to a higher incidence of accidents on the roads, leading to the invention of new medical assistance techniques to repair damaged bodies.
