Using ships to transport goods is by far the most efficient method of moving large amounts of people and goods across the oceans.
The work of Marines has traditionally been to ensure that your country maintains the ability to move people and goods, while at the same time interfering with your enemy's ability to move their people and their goods.
One of the most important recent examples was World War II North-Atlantic campaign. Americans were trying to displace men and materials across the Atlantic Ocean to help Britain.
Contemporaneously, the German navy, using mainly submarines, tried to stop this traffic.
If the Allies had failed, it is likely that Germany would have been able to conquer Britain, just as it did with the rest of Europe.
It seems that goats were first domesticated about 10,000 years ago in Zagros mountain range in Iran.
Cultures and ancient tribes began domesticating them to easily dispose of milk, hair, meat and skin.
Domestic goats were usually maintained in herds that grazed in hills or other areas of pasture, often guarded by shepherds who used to be children or teenagers. These grazing methods are still used today.
Wagons were built in England around the 16th century.
Although the tracks consisted only of parallel wooden planks, they stretched the wagons with horses to reach greater speeds and tighten larger loads in the most difficult parts.
The crossings were introduced quite soon to keep the tracks in place. However, it was soon seen that the tracks would be more efficient if they had an iron deck on them.
This became common practice, but iron wore more the wooden wheels of wagons.
In the end, wooden wheels were replaced by iron wheels. The first completely iron rails were introduced in 1767.
