After its adoption by Congress on July 4, a hand-written draft was sent, signed by Congress President John Hancock and Secretary Charles Thompson at John Dunlap's printing press located a few streets away.
During the night there were between 150 and 200 copies, which today are known as "Dunlap flyers".
The first public reading of the document was conducted by John Nixon in the courtyard of the Independence Hall on July 8.
One was sent to George Washington on July 6, which made him read to his troops in New York on July 9. A copy arrived in London on August 10.
Dunlap's 25 leaflets that are still known to exist are the oldest surviving copies of this document. The original handwritten copy has not survived.
