Virtual teams are subject to the same standards of excellence as conventional teams, but with some small differences.
Members of a virtual group usually function as a point of contact for their immediate physical group.
They tend to have more autonomy than conventional team members, since their teams can meet according to different time zones that local management may not comprehend.
The presence of a true "invisible team" (Larson and LaFasto, 1989, p.109) is also a unique component of a virtual team.
"Invisible team" is the administrative team to which each of the members is addressed. The invisible team sets the standards for each member.
