Kruger National Park is located in north-east South Africa and runs through the border from Mozambique to the east, and that of Zimbabwe to the north, and the Crocodile River marks the southern border.
The park covers 19,500 km2 and is divided into 14 different ecozones, each assisting diverse wildlife.
It is one of the most important attractions in South Africa and is considered the reference of the South African national parks (SANParks).
As with all South African national parks, you have to pay a daily conservation and entrance fee.
It can also be beneficial to buy a Wild Card, which allows entry well to a selection of South African parks, either in all the National Parks of South Africa.
