The vast majority urban development of Hong Kong Island is densely populated into land gained from the sea along the north coast.
This is the place that the British colonizers occupied, therefore, it is a good place to start if you are looking for evidence of the colonial past of the territory.
The Sundarbans are the world's largest coastal mangrove belt, extending 80 km (50 mi) into the interior of Bangladesh and India from the coast.
UNESCO has declared the Sundarbans a World Heritage Site. The part of the forest within Indian territory is called Sundarbans National Park.
The forests are not only mangrove swamps ◗ but include some of the last remaining stops of the powerful jungles that at some point had covered the gangetic plain.
The Sundarbans cover an area of 3,850 km2, of which about one-third is covered with water/swampy areas.
Since 1966, the Sundarbans have become a sanctuary of wildlife, and it is estimated that in the area there are now 400 royal Bengal tigers and about 30,000 axis.
