The Oyapock River Bridge is an atirantado bridge. It extends along the Oyapock River to link the cities of Oiapoque to Brazil and Saint-Georges del Oyapock to French Guiana.
The two towers have a height of 83 meters, is 378 meters long and has two lanes 3.50 meters wide.
The vertical open space under the bridge is 15 meters. Although the construction was completed in August 2011, it was not possible to travel there until March 2017.
The bridge is expected to be fully functional in September 2017, when Brazil's customs are expected to be ready.
The Guaranís were the most important indigenous group that inhabited what is now eastern Paraguay, and lived as semi-nomadic hunters who also practiced subsistence agriculture.
The Chaco region was home to other groups of indigenous tribes such as the Guaycurú and Payaguá, who survived hunting, harvesting and fishing.
Paraguay, formerly called "The Giant Province of the Indies", was created in the 16th century as a result of the encounter between Spanish conquerors and groups of indigenous natives.
Spaniards began the period of colonization that lasted three centuries.
