Japanese labor culture is more hierarchical and formal than Westerners are used to seeing.
Dresses are the standard attire of businesses, and co-workers are called because of their family names or because of their job titles.
Peace in the workplace is crucial, emphasizing the group's effort rather than congratulating individual achievements.
Workers often need the approval of their superiors for whatever decision they make, and are expected to obey the instructions of their superiors without questioning them.
